  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1208 PM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nUpper-level high pressure will continue to slowly slide toward \nthe ECONUS through mid-week. This places much of Texas under a \nSW'ly flow aloft. Meanwhile, an upper-level low across the Desert\nSouthwest will slowly lift to the NE, with its forward momentum \nslowed down by its interaction with a secondary trough across the \nPacific Northwest. Having this slowdown will allow shortwave \ndisturbances to pivot around the aforementioned low as it enters \nthe Great Basin, and southwesterly flow will allow upper-level \nenergy and Pacific moisture from Hurricane Vance (eastern Pacific \nbasin) to advect into the region at the same time. All that to \nsay, rain chances return to South Texas! \n\nSunday night, PWATs will briefly bottom out to 1.0-1.2\" but\nMonday afternoon is when the PWAT values start climbing back up. \nGlobal ensembles are maxing out values Tuesday at 1.7-1.9\", but \nit's not out of the realm of possibility to see even higher PWAT \nvalues given the influence from Vance. All this to say, starting \nMonday night, we're in for a wetter period. Should the trend of \nPWATs trend wetter, it is possible we see accumulations reach 1-2 \ninches in the strongest showers/storms, but the general consensus \nwill be near half-inch amounts. Anyone outdoors on Tuesday may \nneed to reconsider events or move activities indoors.\n\nThis will also keep temperatures cooler than what NBM guidance is\nsuggesting (ended up knocking the temperatures down given the\nrain/widespread cloud cover Tuesday). We remain in the 80s for\nhighs throughout the forecast period.\n\nIf that wasn't enough, we're closely monitoring the guidance for\ncoastal flooding concerns. With the proximity of the full moon and\nan increase in wave periods, water levels are forecast to approach\n1.7 to 2.0 ft above MSL for the high tide cycles from Monday\nthrough at least mid-week. With high tide in that period occurring\ndaylight/evening, an advisory will likely be needed.",
